import QtQuick
import QtQuick.Shapes
import "Platform.js" as Platform

/**
 * A machine's operating system, drawn as its own logo in the interface's ink.
 *
 * The counterpart of {@link Glyph}: the same 24-unit grid and the same single
 * tone, so a logo sits beside an icon without either looking imported.
 *
 * ## Where the drawings come from
 *
 * The same place the web panel's do, character for character. Every mark is the
 * vendor's real logo from **Simple Icons** (CC0 1.0, no attribution required),
 * except Windows and the plain monitor, which come from **Bootstrap Icons**
 * (MIT, Copyright © 2019-2024 The Bootstrap Authors) — Microsoft asked to be
 * removed from Simple Icons, and a monitor belongs to no vendor at all.
 *
 * A logo reconstructed from memory is recognisable only to whoever
 * reconstructed it, so these are the published marks. Path data is source,
 * so nothing is fetched to draw a machine card offline and there is no asset
 * file to track.
 *
 * The strings are normalised before they land here — absolute, no shorthand,
 * arcs expanded to cubics — because Qt misreads the packed arc syntax those sets
 * ship and drew a mangled Debian, Alpine and Tux from the verbatim data. The
 * web panel carries the same normalised strings, so all four renderers agree.
 *
 * ## The one thing that differs from the web
 *
 * `ShapePath` fills with the odd-even rule by default; SVG fills with non-zero.
 * These paths are authored for non-zero — it is what carves Tux's belly and the
 * robot's eyes out of a silhouette — so every one of them says
 * `WindingFill` explicitly. Without it the marks come out as lace.
 *
 * ## Why they are monochrome
 *
 * Colour in this interface means state — a green dot for a connected machine,
 * amber for inference, rose for one that cannot run agents. A vendor colour
 * borrows that vocabulary without meaning it: Apple's flat grey is the disabled
 * grey and Android's green is the online green, so a Mac beside a phone read as
 * *off* beside *on*. They are all drawn in `tone`.
 *
 * Which mark to draw comes from `Platform.osKey`, which mirrors the web app's
 * matcher. The clean-name half lives in `MachineIdentity::productDisplayName()`:
 * it sends a short product *type* rather than a version-bearing sentence, so a
 * new macOS point release does not silently break the icon.
 */
Item {
    id: root

    /** `machineSummary.osName`, or empty on a machine that has never reported. */
    property string osName: ""
    property real size: 20
    /** The one tone every mark is drawn in. */
    property color tone: Theme.textPrimary

    implicitWidth: size
    implicitHeight: size

    readonly property string key: Platform.osKey(root.osName)

    Shape {
        id: shape

        anchors.fill: parent
        antialiasing: true

        /*
          The curve renderer, which draws a diagonal at any scale without the
          stair-stepping the geometry renderer leaves on one.

          Turned on from here rather than written as `preferredRendererType:`
          because both that property and the `Shape.CurveRenderer` value arrived
          in Qt 6.6, and this application builds against 6.4 — the floor that
          decides which glibc the released Linux build needs, and so which
          machines it runs on at all. A declarative line naming a property an
          older Qt does not have is not a warning, it is a file that will not
          load, and this one is drawn on every machine card: on 6.4 the whole
          dashboard went with it. Guarded, an older Qt draws these with the
          geometry renderer and nothing else changes.
        */
        Component.onCompleted: {
            if (Shape.CurveRenderer !== undefined)
                shape.preferredRendererType = Shape.CurveRenderer;
        }

        // Authored on a 24-unit grid; scaled to whatever the caller asked for.
        transform: Scale {
            xScale: root.size / 24
            yScale: root.size / 24
        }

        /*
          One ShapePath per mark, gated on the key.

